S5-07 counted canonical page-fault event exact replays — 2026-11-17

Purpose and source
------------------
Capture the complete successfully delivered #PF event lists for the two source
runs in common-ID series `s5-07-counted-canonical-n2-20261117-a` using the
existing `--execution-observations-out` sidecar. The replays used the exact
nonce and guest inputs/configuration from each corresponding source run; no
additional RDTSC/quantum trace or randomization options were enabled.

Canonical input hashes were revalidated by each runner:

* kernel: `9f55ef7253055a9cf68f8a04323c93dead2fca617386d842fbde9ca7b178b27e`
* initramfs: `ee9183a4eded3c16a8c6a7a81c6d5bb79be8bb023f793f7cd61694ea2bd3e588`
* ACPI blob: `b1ba6078a4d9bc7a8d224980713d8242e4a708743ce4f56093b00a07c631ffaf`
* ACPI manifest: `2bd690ae0817705b1b128675f9628dd7212fee74ab97c88da3599a1ae8e398f2`

Both were local macOS/aarch64 release-runner 0.1.0 runs on source revision
`dd7712141f582117cf0ab2c2fa28e6b44f9646d5`, with Stage4A, CPL0 set, user mode,
RAM 128 MiB and budget 3,000,000,000. The series profile remains
`smoke_not_acceptance`.

Replay comparability and capture completeness
---------------------------------------------
Each replay exactly matches its corresponding source `stop` JSON object,
including the COM1 hex payload, and the decoded COM1 bytes exactly match the
source `uart.bin`. Nonce and acceptance also match. Each observations sidecar
has schema 13, `write_error=null`, and a runner-recorded byte count and SHA-256
matching the file.

| Run | Nonce | Source/replay PF total | Events | Omitted | Fault walks present |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0001 | `OD518e8be9ac44221a00000fb3400000000` | 33 / 33 | 33 | 0 | 33 |
| 0002 | `OD518e8bfb13a39d1980000fb3400000001` | 34 / 34 | 34 | 0 | 34 |

The collector retains up to 256 delivered #PF events. The observed 33 and 34
events are complete: the array lengths equal both sidecar and source stop
totals, with zero omissions. Each event includes delivery-order RIP, CR2,
error code, access, CPL, pre-delivery context and the optional original fault
page walk; all page walks in these replays are present.

Fault event alignment
---------------------
The delivered-event sequences were aligned by the tuple
`(RIP, CR2, error_code, access, CPL)`. The first 30 events match in order. Run 2
has one inserted event at its zero-based index 30; the three following event
tuples match run 1 indices 30–32 and run 2 indices 31–33. Thus the one-event
count delta is localized in the recorded sequence; this does not classify the
variation as benign or prove nonce causality. Some later matched-event context
fields differ, including CR3, user RSP, frame pointers and page-table physical
addresses, so full observation payloads are not identical.

The inserted run 2 event is:

* RIP `0xffffffff8271a9c1`, CPL0, write, CR2 `0x7fff9d9d7429`, error code `0x2`.
* The original access walk has present PML4/PDPT/PD entries and a raw zero PT
  leaf at index 471: `NotPresent { level: Pt }`.
* Fault context records RDI=CR2, RCX=7, RSI=`0xffffffff82fdeb8f`.
* The return chain reaches `create_elf_tables` in `binfmt_elf.c:206`, then
  `load_elf_binary`, `kernel_execve`, `run_init_process`, and `kernel_init`.

The matching debug ELF is BuildID
`338e542e384c46228f7a378babc0f9bb2b6a5997`, SHA-256
`7e460f048c3de0f8ee85fd326f2f65d95a6ee448e57b6f5333e2be79b5be9d1f`.
`llvm-dwarfdump --lookup` maps the fault RIP to `rep_movs_alternative+0x11` in
`copy_user_64.S:47`; the bytes at the RIP are `88 07` (`movb %al,(%rdi)`). The
debug ELF bytes at RSI decode to `x86_64\0`. This is the seven-byte platform
string copied by `_copy_to_user` into the initial `/init` user stack at
`arch_align_stack(...) - 7`. CR2 and the later initial CPL3 RSP
`0x7fff9d9d7270` are on the same 4 KiB page; the seven-byte write itself does
not cross a page boundary.

This localizes the extra successfully delivered #PF to initial ELF stack
population by the kernel. It does not explain why that target page was absent
in run 2 but no corresponding fault occurred in run 1. The different user stack
addresses, CR3 values and page-table addresses are observations, not a proven
cause. No comparator/allowlist change follows.

Commands
--------
The two invocations were identical except for nonce/output paths; flags below
show the shared command shape:

```sh
target/release/od-runner linux-direct \
  --vmlinuz work/stage4/linux-7.0.0-38.38/vmlinuz-7.0.0-38-generic \
  --initramfs work/stage4/s5-07-canonical-initramfs-rebuild/initramfs.cpio \
  --acpi-blob work/stage5/staging/s5-07-canonical-acpi-20261117.bin \
  --expected-vmlinuz-sha256 9f55ef7253055a9cf68f8a04323c93dead2fca617386d842fbde9ca7b178b27e \
  --expected-initramfs-sha256 ee9183a4eded3c16a8c6a7a81c6d5bb79be8bb023f793f7cd61694ea2bd3e588 \
  --expected-acpi-manifest-hash 2bd690ae0817705b1b128675f9628dd7212fee74ab97c88da3599a1ae8e398f2 \
  --ram-mib 128 --run --budget 3000000000 --set-cpl0 \
  --cpuid-profile stage4a --user-mode --nonce <source-run-nonce> \
  --out <replay-runner-json> --execution-observations-out <sidecar-json>
```

